Z||000030||Monkeyman||02-20-2000||05:20 AM||||I keep seeing the high mileages on everyones cars.  What has everyone done to get 150,000 miles or more with out a rebuild?  Just basic maintainance and lots of love?  My '86 coupe has 100k on it and it's on it's last legs but it hasn't been taken care of.  I'll either have to replace the motor in my '85 GT or have it rebuilt (too expensive either way), then I hope to get 150k out of it.||||reg||1
Z||000031||batboy||02-20-2000||12:26 PM||||Regular maintenance and oil changes are critical to an engine's longevity. My vehicles see mostly highway miles and I rack up the miles so fast that it seem like I'm always changing oil on one or the other. I use a 5,000 mile interval because it's easy to remember. Vehicles that get used in heavy city traffic or in dusty areas (lots of gravel road cruising) probably should have their oil changed more frequently. Most regular oil will last over 10,000 miles before they start breaking down nowadays, but I've found that the oil gets way too dirty if you run them that long. Dirty oil causes additional wear on internal engine parts and creates sludge which could eventually block oil passages.||||reg||1
Z||000032||batboy||02-20-2000||12:32 PM||||BTW, my Suburban has 266,000 miles on it with the original engine and it's still running. Maybe not running like new and it does use a couple quarts of oil between changes, but it's still running. I'm the second owner and I've abused this truck by towing and four wheeling. It gets a lot of winter driving use which is also hard on engines, especially cold starts in sub-zero weather, which thankfully we don't get much of here.||||reg||1
